        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Iberians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The eastern and southern coasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>: today’s Catalonia, eastern and northern Aragon, the Valencian Community, the Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and perhaps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; key sites <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castellet-de-banyoles">Castellet de Banyoles</a> at Tivissa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ullastret">Ullastret</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucentum">Lucentum</a>, Pozo Moro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elche">Elche</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Iberian culture from the 6th century BCE, perhaps with far older roots; the Iberian language spoken from the 7th to at least the 1st century BCE; an aristocracy and oppida from the late 5th and early 4th centuries BCE; Carthaginian conquest south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> under the Barcids, then Roman conquest during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>; the language extinct by the 1st to 2nd centuries CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">oppida and chieftains bound to followers by fides; sculpture such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche">Lady of Elche</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-baza">Lady of Baza</a>; pottery painted with red geometric designs; the falcata and other iron weapons; soldiers and cavalry serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; the Iberian semi-syllabic scripts</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Greek and Roman writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Avienius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a>; excavations of settlements, sanctuaries and tombs; Iberian inscriptions in three scripts, in a language still unclassified</span></div>
        </div>
