    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Iberians</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iberians.jpg" alt="Iberians" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Iberians <span class="kid-tale-cr">Heparina1985, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 2,600 years ago, Greek writers told of the Iberians of Spain’s eastern and southern coasts.</p><p>The whole Iberian Peninsula is named after them.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silver.jpg" alt="Silver" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silver <span class="kid-tale-cr">Alchemist-hp, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</span></figcaption></figure><p>They lived in villages and in walled towns, led by chieftains and nobles.</p><p>They farmed, bred horses and dug silver, iron, tin and copper from the ground.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-of-baza.jpg" alt="Lady of Baza" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lady of Baza <span class="kid-tale-cr">Relanzón, Santiago (photo), CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They worshipped many gods, such as an earth goddess linked with birds, flowers and wheat.</p><p>They burned their dead and laid the ashes in urns inside stone tombs.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche.jpg" alt="Lady of Elche" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lady of Elche <span class="kid-tale-cr">Luis Fernández García, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Iberian artists who knew Greek art well carved stone statues such as the Lady of Elche.</p><p>They painted pots with red patterns and wrote their language in scripts of their own.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="Hannibal" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Hannibal <span class="kid-tale-cr">Fratelli Alinari, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Hannibal of Carthage conquered many Iberians, and most of them then fought for Carthage against Rome.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ullastret.jpg" alt="Ullastret" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Ullastret <span class="kid-tale-cr">Josep Renalias, CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Rome won that war, and over the years the Iberian language gave way to Latin.</p><p>The ruins of their towns, such as Ullastret, are still being uncovered and studied.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In the narrow sense, the Iberians were the pre-Indo-European peoples of the eastern and southern coasts of the peninsula that bears their name, distinct from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and Celtiberians of its interior.</li><li>From the late 5th century BCE their societies centred on fortified towns, oppida, led by aristocracies and by chieftains bound to their followers by a tie the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called fides.</li><li>Iberian soldiers fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and for both sides in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, after which their lands became Roman provinces and their language gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, the Iberians sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 324 BCE, along with embassies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, Italics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, to ask for his friendship.</p></aside>
    </section>

